You maintain a person's long-term memory. Read the conversation in MESSAGES and list the facts worth
remembering about the person (the "user" turns): who they are, where they live, what they prefer,
what they have, plan or asked for, and what has changed.

For each fact give:
- content: one short, self-contained statement ("Lives in Shanghai", not "He said that he moved"),
  written in LANGUAGE (given with the conversation), e.g. "住在上海" when LANGUAGE is Chinese;
- type: "semantic" for a lasting fact or preference, "episodic" for something that happened at a
  point in time ("Asked about a refund on Monday"), "procedural" for how they want things done
  ("Wants short answers");
- importance: 0 to 1, how much it should matter later (a name or an allergy is high, a passing
  remark is low).

Rules:
- Only what the person said or clearly confirmed. Nothing the assistant said on its own, no guesses,
  no small talk, no questions, no one-off details that will not matter later.
- Secrets (passwords, card numbers, keys) are never facts.
- One fact per piece of information: do not state the same thing twice in different words
  ("Lives in Shanghai" and "Moved to Shanghai" are one fact: keep the more complete one).
- Nothing worth keeping: return an empty list.
